\subsection{Linear Projection: Pipeline Complexity Determines Onset}
\label{sec:linear_results}

$\pi_0$~\cite{black2024pi0} (3.3B parameters, 256 tokens, linear projection) uses a flow matching action head~\cite{lipman2023flow}; we evaluate pruning robustness via offline normalized L1 action loss rather than LIBERO success rate.\footnote{Offline action loss measures single-step prediction quality; closed-loop evaluation, where errors compound, may reveal additional degradation. We report offline results as preliminary evidence pending live evaluation.} At $k{=}0.1$ (retaining only 10\% of tokens), the normalized loss is 0.976, a 2.4\% \emph{improvement} over the unpruned baseline ($\mathcal{L}_1 < 1.0$ indicates lower loss than the full-token reference). At $k{=}0.5$ the loss remains 0.983. Token removal at these ratios does not degrade offline action prediction quality.

X-VLA~\cite{zheng2025xvla} (880M parameters, 50 tokens) is nominally a linear projection model but routes vision tokens through a 12-layer BART encoder~\cite{lewis2020bart} before the LLM, creating a more complex effective pipeline. The baseline achieves 99.2\% SR (496/500). At $k{=}0.95$ SR remains 99.0\% ($-0.2$pp), and at $k{=}0.9$ it holds at 97.6\% ($-1.6$pp). However, at $k{=}0.7$ SR drops to 56.2\% (281/500), a 43-percentage-point cliff from $k{=}0.9$. Partial results at lower retention ratios show continued degradation: $\sim$42\% at $k{=}0.5$, $\sim$32\% at $k{=}0.3$, and $\sim$0\% at $k{=}0.1$.\footnote{Values at $k \leq 0.5$ are partial (4 of 10 tasks complete); final numbers may shift by several percentage points.} X-VLA thus exhibits a distinct failure mode: robust under mild pruning ($k \geq 0.9$), then a delayed cliff at $k{=}0.7$ followed by graded decline. This pattern differs from both $\pi_0$'s immunity and OpenVLA's smooth degradation (49.0\% at $k{=}0.7$). The BART encoder introduces cross-token mixing that creates partial dependencies (CV $= 0.151$, between $\pi_0$'s 0.038 and OpenVLA's 0.215), explaining the intermediate onset point.

The $\pi_0$--X-VLA contrast within the nominal ``linear projection'' class demonstrates that the connector label alone is insufficient: the effective vision-to-LLM pipeline complexity determines pruning tolerance. $\pi_0$'s single linear layer preserves full token independence (CV $= 0.038$), while X-VLA's BART encoder introduces enough cross-token structure to shift the degradation onset inward.

The controlled comparison between $\pi_0$ and OpenVLA remains the strongest evidence for connector-determined compressibility. Both use 256 vision tokens from a SigLIP~\cite{zhai2023siglip} encoder; they differ only in the connector (linear projection vs.\ 3-layer MLP). At $k{=}0.1$, $\pi_0$ improves by 2.4\% while OpenVLA degrades by 61\%. With token count, encoder architecture, and pruning protocol held constant, the 63-percentage-point gap is attributable to the connector alone.

